\begin{proof}
Let $\mathcal{T}=\bigcap_\alpha \mathcal{T}_\alpha$. The intersection of any family of topologies on $X$ is a topology on $X$, since it contains $\varnothing$ and $X$, is closed under arbitrary unions, and is closed under finite intersections. Clearly, $\mathcal{T}\subseteq\mathcal{T}_\alpha$ for every $\alpha$. If $\mathcal{O}$ is any topology on $X$ contained in every $\mathcal{T}_\alpha$, then $\mathcal{O}\subseteq\bigcap_\alpha\mathcal{T}_\alpha=\mathcal{T}$. Thus $\mathcal{T}$ is the largest topology contained in all the $\mathcal{T}_\alpha$. A largest element is unique.
\end{proof}

\begin{proof}
Set $\mathcal{T}=\bigcap_\alpha\mathcal{T}_\alpha$. Since intersections preserve common members and each $\mathcal{T}_\alpha$ is a topology, $\mathcal{T}$ contains $\varnothing$ and $X$, and is closed under arbitrary unions and finite intersections. Thus $\mathcal{T}$ is a topology on $X$.

For each $\alpha$, the definition of intersection gives $\mathcal{T}\subseteq\mathcal{T}_\alpha$. If $\mathcal{O}$ is any topology on $X$ with $\mathcal{O}\subseteq\mathcal{T}_\alpha$ for every $\alpha$, then every member of $\mathcal{O}$ lies in every $\mathcal{T}_\alpha$, so $\mathcal{O}\subseteq\mathcal{T}$. Therefore $\mathcal{T}$ is the largest topology on $X$ contained in all the $\mathcal{T}_\alpha$, and its uniqueness follows immediately.
\end{proof}
